  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\n\nRain will be ongoing Thursday morning as chances remain at 80 to \n90%. Most locations will see totals of a half inch, while a few \nspots could see up to an inch, particularly across portions of \nnortheast Nebraska. PWATs still appear to be on the higher end of \nclimatology, with guidance ranging from 1 to 1.5 inches which is at \nor above the 90th percentile of KOAX sounding climatology for that \ndate. Rain gradually comes to an end from west to east by Thursday \nevening as the H5 disturbance moves east and gets absorbed into a \nstrong H5 wave that ejects from Saskatchewan/Manitoba into the \nNorthern Plains.\n\nDry conditions are largely anticipated through at least Sunday \nas H5 ridging moves in. Expect a cool Friday with highs in the \n50s before a warm up to the upper 60s and low 70s occurs for the\nweekend. Friday night into Saturday will see a strong sfc high \nmove over most of the Plains region, resulting in clear skies \nand allowing us to cool to the low to mid 30s, so don't be \nsurprised if you see some frost. Guidance suggests another \npotential wave approaching the area by late Sunday into Monday,\nbut with this forecast update, the NBM suggests dry conditions \nremaining in place.",
